# The orphan findings ledger is append-only: every branch adds its own
# block at the end and nothing edits another's. Git's default merge treats
# two appends at the same offset as a conflict, so every branch that
# recorded a finding had to hand-resolve the same non-conflict — several
# times a day once two sessions are working at once.
#
# `union` keeps both sides' lines, which for an append-only ledger is not a
# compromise but the correct answer: losing either block would be losing a
# recorded finding.
docs/reviews/orphan-findings.md merge=union

# The finding registry is append-only AND hash-chained, and those two facts
# together manufacture conflicts that carry no information. A branch appends
# its findings at the tail; upstream's closure-reconcile lane marks past rows
# RESOLVED, which forces a rechain of every row after the earliest closure.
# Git then sees a rewritten hunk ending on the last line and an append
# beginning on the next one — two adjacent edits, no semantic conflict. On
# 2026-09-11 that cost the same hand resolution on twenty stacked branches,
# in the one file where a hand slip is indistinguishable from tampering.
#
# `union` cannot do it (it would keep both the pre- and post-closure copies
# of 895 rows) and `ours` cannot (it would discard upstream's closures), so
# this one gets a driver that knows the invariant: upstream's prefix, then
# this branch's tail, rechained and verified — and a refusal, with real
# conflict markers, for anything the invariant does not make unambiguous
# (a deleted or reordered row, the same finding decided differently on both
# sides, a duplicate id). See tools/gates/finding-registry-merge.ts.
docs/reviews/_registry/findings.jsonl merge=findings-registry
